
###########################################
#####   Description of Sample Cases   #####
###########################################

NOTE: If wanting to run GFR in serial, ignore [MPI COMMAND] and simply enter:

        prompt> gfr [INPUT FILE]

      where [INPUT FILE] is replaced with the name of the GFR input file.

NOTE: If wanting to run GFR in parallel, replace [MPI COMMAND] with the
      necessary command to run a program using MPI. A common command would be:

        [MPI COMMAND] => mpiexec -np [# of MPI PROCESSES]

      where [# of MPI PROCESSES] should be an integer value for the desired 
      number of MPI processes. For example, to run GFR using 4 MPI processes:

        prompt> mpiexec -np 4 gfr [INPUT FILE]

      where [INPUT FILE] is replaced with the name of the GFR input file.

NOTE: When running in parallel, best performance is achieved if the average
      number of cells per MPI process is larger than 20 for 2D and 10 for 3D.

NOTE: Unless stated otherwise, all "Runtime" performance numbers were found
      running the problem on a workstation with dual Intel Xeon 6230R CPUs,
      each with 26 physical cores (52 total), and 128 GB of memory. These
      performance numbers are provided to hopefully give you an approximate of
      how long each problem might take to complete compared to your available
      computational resources. Of course, any adjustments to the input file
      affecting the solution order or grid resolution for a problem will also
      have an effect on the runtime for a problem.

################################################################################

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%   Isentropic Euler Vortex   %%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

 Input File : Isentropic_Vortex/euler_vortex.in
  Grid File : [Generated internally within GFR]

Recommended
 # of MPI
 processes  : 1-50

Description : This test case concerns the long term transport of a vortex by a
              uniform base flow over a 2D doubly periodic domain. This problem
              is aimed at testing a high-order method’s capability to preserve
              vorticity in an unsteady inviscid flow.

              The grid resolution for this test case can be adjusted by changing
              the 'VortexGrid_DOF' input parameter which specifies the
              degrees-of-freedom (DoF) in each direction. The number of grid
              cells in each direction is determined as:
                 [# of cells] = nint( VortexGrid_DOF / (solution_order+1) )
              Example: VortexGrid_DOF = 200
                       solution_order = 5
                       Grid = 33x33 Cells

    Runtime : This case reached its final simulation time in
              about 30 seconds using 52 MPI processes.

How to run this case:
              prompt> cd Isentropic_Vortex
              prompt> [MPI COMMAND] gfr euler_vortex.in

################################################################################

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%   Isentropic Euler Vortex (Circular Grid Version)   %%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

      Input File  : Isentropic_Vortex_Circular-Grid/vortex_circular-grid.in
       Grid Files : grid_files/vortex_circular-grid.64.p3d
                    grid_files/vortex_circular-grid.128.p3d
 Plot3D  BC File  : grid_files/vortex_circular-grid.bcs.p3d
 Plot3D Cut File  : grid_files/vortex_circular-grid.cut.p3d

Recommended
 # of MPI
 processes  : 1- 50 for *.64.p3d
              1-150 for *.128.p3d

Description : This test case is the very similar to the previous isentropic
              Euler vortex test case with a few changes:
                1) This version uses a circular 5-block Plot3D grid file with an
                   O-H topology. Since this grid contains curvature, a P4
                   agglomeration is used to combine linear grid cells into
                   high-order grid cells, i.e., 4x4 (16) linear grid cells are
                   combined into one P4 grid cell.
                2) This version uses a stationary vortex that remains seated at
                   the coordinate origin. This is mostly because the curved
                   domain boundary does not allow for periodic boundaries
                   required to propagate the vortex multiple times through the
                   grid domain. However, the use of characteristic boundaries
                   allows this version of the problem to run indefinitely since
                   the error created by the numerical scheme is allowed to leave
                   the domain, whereas the fully periodic version will
                   eventually accumulate enough error where it coalesces,
                   becoming a flow feature in itself, and destroys the vortex.
                3) The solution order for this version is P7 (8th order),
                   whereas the previous version is P3 (4th order).
              
              There are two grid files that can be used with this test case:
                 A) vortex_circular-grid.64.p3d which contains 64 linear cells
                    per quadrant in the rotational direction.
                 B) vortex_circular-grid.128.p3d which contains 128 linear cells
                    per quadrant in the rotational direction.

              If you want to make a DoF comparison between this version and the
              previous fully periodic version:
                1) For the previous version, use the "VortexGrid_DOF" input
                   variable to control the how many DoF to use for that version.
                2) Using the two available grid files for this version, you
                   can change the solution order using either the
                   "Solution_Order" or "All_Orders" input variables to get the
                   following equivalent DoF:
                      P   vortex_*.64.p3d   vortex_*.128.p3d
                     ----------------------------------------------------
                      1          55               111
                      2          83               166
                      3         111               222
                      4         139               277
                      5         166               333
                      6         194               388
                      7         222               443

    Runtime : This case reached its final simulation time in
              about 100 seconds using 52 MPI processes.

How to run this case:
              prompt> cd Isentropic_Vortex_Circular-Grid
              prompt> [MPI COMMAND] gfr vortex_circular-grid.in

################################################################################

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%   Inviscid Subsonic Flow over a Gaussian Bump   %%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Input File  : Inviscid_Gaussian_Bump/bump.in
 Grid Files : grid_files/bump.P1.msh
              grid_files/bump.P2.msh
              grid_files/bump.P3.msh
              grid_files/bump.P4.msh

Recommended
 # of MPI
 processes  : 1-16

Description : This test case concerns the subsonic flow over a smooth Gaussian
              bump in a channel. This problem is aimed at testing high-order
              methods for the computation of internal flow with a high-order
              curved boundary representation. In this subsonic flow problem, the
              geometry is smooth, and so is the flow.

              All grid files contain the same number of grid cells (256), and
              the names of the grid files refer to the order of the cells
              contained in the grid. The 'bump.P1.msh' grid contains linear (P1)
              cells, the 'bump.P2.msh' grid contains quadratic (P2) cells, the
              'bump.P3.msh' grid contains cubic (P3) cells, and the
              'bump.P4.msh' grid contains quartic (P4) cells.

              This test case can be used to experiment with how the grid order
              affects solution accuracy for various solution orders. It is
              recommended that the solution order is greater than or equal to
              the grid order.

    Runtime : This case reached convergence in just over 10 minutes running
              in serial, and roughly 60 seconds using 16 MPI processes.

How to run this case:
              prompt> cd Inviscid_Gaussian_Bump
              prompt> [MPI COMMAND] gfr bump.in

################################################################################

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%   Couette Flow   %%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

     Input File : Couette_Flow/couette.in
      Grid File : grid_files/couette.grd
 Plot3D BC File : grid_files/couette.bcs.grd

Recommended
 # of MPI
 processes  : 1-2

    Description : This is the classic Couette flow problem. It consists of 2D
                  viscous flow in a channel between two parallel walls, with one
                  wall moving at a constant velocity while the other wall stays
                  still. In this specific case, the two walls are treated as
                  isothermal walls with a constant temperature of 300 K, and the
                  top wall has a velocity of 30.0 m/s.

    Runtime : This case reached convergence in about 10 seconds
              running in serial.

How to run this case:
              prompt> cd Couette_Flow
              prompt> [MPI COMMAND] gfr couette.in

################################################################################

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%   Taylor-Green Vortex Problem   %%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

 Input File : Taylor-Green_Vortex/tgv.in
  Grid File : [Generated internally within GFR]

Recommended
 # of MPI
 processes  : 8-400

Description : This problem is the LES/DNS of a three-dimensional periodic and
              transitional flow defined by a simple initial condition: the
              Taylor-Green vortex. The computational domain is a triply periodic
              cubic domain, in which initially 8 vortices reside, described by
              an analytical formula. This flow transitions to turbulence, with
              the creation of small scales, followed by a decay phase similar to
              decaying homogeneous turbulence.

              This test case will create an output file named
              'TaylorGreen_KEDR.dat' which provides the integrated kinetic
              energy and kinetic energy dissipation rates (KEDR) throughout the
              simulation. This test case will also create an output solution
              file named 'CGNS_files/TGV_vorticity_8s.cgns' which contains the
              vorticity norm on the periodic face x/L=-PI at t*=8. These two
              results can be compared with a reference solution found by a
              pseudo-spectral code using 512^3 degrees of freedom (DOF) which
              can be downloaded from:
         https://www.grc.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/sites/22/C3.3_datafiles.zip

              The grid resolution for this test case can be adjusted by changing
              the 'VortexGrid_DOF' input parameter which specifies the DOF in
              each direction. The number of grid cells in each direction is
              determined as:
                 [# of cells] = nint( VortexGrid_DOF / (solution_order+1) )
              Example: VortexGrid_DOF = 128
                       solution_order = 4
                       Grid = 26x26x26 Cells

    Runtime : Using 52 MPI processes, this case took about 23 minutes to reach
              its final simulation time. Running this case on NAS using 128 MPI
              processes on 1 Rome node (128 cores per node) took 6 minutes to
              reach the final simulation time. Running this case on NAS using
              52 MPI processes on 1 Rome node (128 cores per node) took 15
              minutes to reach the final simulation time.
              This case uses between 10GB-20GB of memory.
              NOTE: Running this in serial will probably take 12+ hours.

How to run this case:
              prompt> cd Taylor-Green_Vortex
              prompt> [MPI COMMAND] gfr tgv.in

################################################################################

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%   Taylor-Couette flow problem using internally generated grid   %%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

 Input File : Taylor-Couette_IG/taylor-couette.in
  Grid File : [Generated internally within GFR]

Recommended
 # of MPI
 processes  : 500-2000

Description : This problem is the LES of a Taylor-Couette flow consisting of
              two concentric cylinders, with the inner cylinder rotating and
              the outer cylinder remaining still. The inner cylinder is set to
              a moving isothermal wall with a specified rotation speed, the 
              outer cylinder is set to a no-slip adiabatic wall, and the
              inflow/outflow boundaries are set to periodic. The flow is
              initialized to an analytic laminar solution for this problem with
              zero axial velocity. A vorticity distribution, inspired by the
              Taylor-Green vortex problem, is superimposed onto the laminar
              solution to quickly transition to a turbulent flow.

              This test case will create an output file named
              'TaylorCouette_KEDR.dat' which provides the integrated kinetic
              energy and kinetic energy dissipation rates (KEDR) throughout the
              simulation. This test case will also create four output solution
              files at t*=[22.058,24.016,27.2,33.5].

              The grid resolution for this test case can be adjusted by changing
              a number of input parameters:
                TCF_Radial_Cells    = [# of cells in the radial direction]
                TCF_Azimuthal_Cells = [# of cells in the azimuthal direction]
                TCF_Axial_Cells     = [# of cells in the axial direction]
                TCF_Inner_Wall_Spacing = [radial spacing of first cell
                                          on inner cylinder wall]
                TCF_Outer_Wall_Spacing = [radial spacing of first cell
                                          on outer cylinder wall]
                TCF_Inner_Radius = [radius of inner cylinder]
                TCF_Outer_Radius = [radius of outer cylinder]

              This version of the problem uses the Optimized-Embedded-Pair ERK
              5-stage/3rd-order(with embedded 2nd-order) method with adaptive
              time stepping size.

        NOTE: The flow becomes fully turbulent around t*=30 and the final
              special output for this case occurs at t*=33.5. The final
              simulation time is set to t*=75; however, any comparisons between
              different orders/resolutions of this problem is nearly impossible
              after t*=35 due to the fully turbulent nature of the flow. This
              means this problem does not need reach its final simulation time
              for a good analysis of the results, and can probably be stopped
              around t*=40. Anything beyond that is just for thorough
              comparisons to previously published results.

    Runtime : It is recommended to run this case on a HPC system. Running this
              case on a local workstation using 52 MPI processes took nearly
              6 DAYS (140 hours) to reach the final simulation time of t*=75.
              Running this case on NAS using 2560 MPI processes across 20 Rome
              nodes (128 cores per node) took 11 minutes to reach t*=10.

How to run this case:
              prompt> cd Taylor-Couette_IG
              prompt> [MPI COMMAND] gfr taylor-couette.in

################################################################################

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%   Taylor-Couette flow problem using high-order CGNS grid   %%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

 Input File : Taylor-Couette_CGNS/taylor-couette.in
  Grid File : grid_files/Taylor-Couette.20x48x24.P3.cgns
                NOTE : This grid file first needs to be decompressed by gzip

Recommended
 # of MPI
 processes  : 500-2000

Description : This is the exact same problem as Taylor-Couette_IG, except this
              version uses a high-order (P3) CGNS grid created by Pointwise,
              with the flow domain of the CGNS grid being exactly half that of
              the internal grid from that problem. The input parameter
                GRID_SCALING_FACTOR = 2.0
              is used to scale this CGNS grid to the same dimensions as the
              version using the internal grid.

              This version of the problem uses the Strong-Stability-Preserving
              ERK 3-stage/3rd-order method with CFL based time stepping.

    Runtime : It is recommended to run this case on a HPC system. Running this
              case on a local workstation using 52 MPI processes took nearly
              6 DAYS (136 hours) to reach the final simulation time of t*=75.
              Running this case on NAS using 2560 MPI processes across 20 Rome
              nodes (128 cores per node) took 11 minutes to reach t*=10.

How to run this case:
              prompt> gunzip grid_files/Taylor-Couette.20x48x24.P3.cgns.gz
              prompt> cd Taylor-Couette_CGNS
              prompt> [MPI COMMAND] gfr taylor-couette.in

################################################################################

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%   LES of Flow over Cylinder at Re=3900   %%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

 Input File : LES_Flow_over_Cylinder/cylinder.in
  Grid File : grid_files/cylinder.P3.msh

Recommended
 # of MPI
 processes  : 16-500

Description : This test case concerns the flow over a cylinder at a Reynolds
              number of 3900. This is based off of the similar test case of
              flow over an infinitely long cylinder at the same flow conditions
              which approximates the infinite span using a spanwise periodic
              mesh. However, periodic boundary conditions for Gmsh grid files
              are not yet supported in GFR. Therefore, the periodic boundary
              conditions at the spanwise grid boundaries have been replaced in
              this version of this test case with symmetric/reflecting boundary
              conditions.

              This case uses a limiter filter to keep the simulation from
              diverging. A limiter filter is a two-part process. The first part
              uses a resolution marker to identify "troubled cells", which are
              cells that show signs of large energy in the highest polynomial
              mode. The second part consists of applying a modal filter to only
              the troubled cells. Inputs for a global filter that is applied
              to ALL cells are also provided (but commented out) in the input
              file. Using the global filter will result in a more diffused
              solution because dissipation will also be added to cells that do
              not need additional dissipation to remain stable.

              This case consists of two phases: the initialization phase and the
              time-averaging phase. The final simulation time is set to t*=800,
              and the start of time averaging is set to t*=100. These numbers
              were taken from the description for this problem given by the
              High-Order CFD Workshops.

       NOTE : The grid provided for this test case is very coarse and not good
              enough to produce a good LES solution. For example, the domain
              span is only 2x the cylinder diameter, there are only 7 cells in
              the spanwise direction, and the spanwise BCs are specified as
              slip walls instead of periodic (periodic BCs are not yet supported
              with Gmsh). This problem is only intended as an example on how to
              set up an LES simulation with time averaging. The solution files
              can be examined to visualize some vortical structures behind the
              cylinder, but these are not results you would want to compare to
              good data. Better quality high-order grids can be found at some
              of the websites for previous High-Order CFD Workshops.
              
    Runtime : Running this case on NAS using 672 MPI processes across
              24 Broadwell nodes (28 cores per node) took 37 minutes to reach
              the final simulation time of t*=800.

How to run this case:
              prompt> cd LES_Flow_over_Cylinder
              prompt> [MPI COMMAND] gfr cylinder.in

